@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Lambda Expressions in Java";
}

<div class="card border-0 shadow-sm">
    <div class="card-body p-4 p-lg-5">

        <div class="mb-4">
            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">Lambda Expressions in Java</h2>
            <p class="lead text-muted mb-0">
                Learn how lambda expressions provide a concise way to represent
                behavior and work with functional interfaces, collections,
                streams, and modern Java APIs.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- INTRODUCTION -->
        <section class="mb-5">
            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">What is a Lambda Expression?</h3>

            <p>
                A lambda expression is a compact representation of behavior
                that can be passed as a value. Lambda expressions are closely
                associated with functional interfaces.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>
                <pre class="mb-0"><code>(parameters) -&gt; expression</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block mt-3">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>
                <pre class="mb-0"><code>(parameters) -&gt; {
    // statements
}</code></pre>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- BASIC LAMBDA -->
        <section class="mb-5">
            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Basic Lambda Expression</h3>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>
                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Runnable task = () -&gt; {


   System.out.println("CIIT Institute background task is running successfully 🏠👍...!");

};

task.run();</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>CIIT Institute background task is running successfully 🏠👍...!</code></pre>
</div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The lambda <code>() -&gt; { ... }</code> provides the
                implementation of the single abstract method of
                <code>Runnable</code>.
            </p>
        </section>

        <!-- PARAMETERS -->
        <section class="mb-5">
            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Lambda Parameters</h3>

            <p>
                Lambda expressions can accept zero, one, or multiple
                parameters.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>
                <pre class="mb-0"><code>var square = (int number) -&gt;
    number * number;

System.out.println(
    square.applyAsInt(5)
);</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>25</code></pre>
</div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                For compatible functional interfaces, parameter types can
                often be inferred.
            </p>
        </section>

        <!-- FUNCTIONAL INTERFACE -->
        <section class="mb-5">
            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Lambda and Functional Interfaces</h3>

            <p>
                A functional interface has exactly one abstract method.
                Lambda expressions can provide implementations for such
                interfaces.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>
                <pre class="mb-0"><code>interface Calculator {

    int calculate(int a, int b);

}

Calculator addition =
    (a, b) -&gt; a + b;

System.out.println(
    addition.calculate(10, 20)
);</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>30</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>

        <!-- BLOCK BODY -->
        <section class="mb-5">
            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Expression Body vs Block Body</h3>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>
                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Calculator add =
    (a, b) -&gt; a + b;</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block mt-3">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>
                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Calculator multiply =
    (a, b) -&gt; {

        int result = a * b;

        return result;
    };</code></pre>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- JAVA UTIL FUNCTION -->
        <section class="mb-5">
            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Built-in Functional Interfaces</h3>

            <p>
                Java provides many functional interfaces in
                <code>java.util.function</code>.
            </p>

            <div class="table-responsive">
                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                    <thead class="table-dark">
                        <tr>
                            <th>Interface</th>
                            <th>Purpose</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>

                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>Predicate&lt;T&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Tests a condition and returns boolean.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>Function&lt;T,R&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Converts an input into an output.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>Consumer&lt;T&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Consumes an input without returning a result.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>Supplier&lt;T&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Supplies a value without taking an input.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>UnaryOperator&lt;T&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Transforms a value into the same type.</td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td><code>BinaryOperator&lt;T&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Combines two values of the same type.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>

                </table>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- PREDICATE -->
        <section class="mb-5">
            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Predicate</h3>

            <p>
                <code>Predicate&lt;T&gt;</code> represents a boolean-valued
                function.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>
                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Predicate&lt;Integer&gt; isEven =
    number -&gt; number % 2 == 0;

System.out.println(
    isEven.test(10)
);</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>true</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>

        <!-- FUNCTION -->
        <section class="mb-5">
            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Function</h3>

            <p>
                <code>Function&lt;T,R&gt;</code> accepts one value and produces
                another value.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>
                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Function&lt;String, Integer&gt; length =
    text -&gt; text.length();

System.out.println(
    length.apply("Java")
);</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>4</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>

        <!-- CONSUMER -->
        <section class="mb-5">
            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Consumer</h3>

            <p>
                <code>Consumer&lt;T&gt;</code> accepts a value and performs an
                operation without returning a result.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>
                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Consumer&lt;String&gt; printer =
    text -&gt; System.out.println(text);

printer.accept("Welcome to CIIT Institute: Learn, Code, and Succeed 👀👨‍🏫...!");
</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Welcome to CIIT Institute: Learn, Code, and Succeed 👀👨‍🏫...!</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>

        <!-- SUPPLIER -->
        <section class="mb-5">
            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Supplier</h3>

            <p>
                <code>Supplier&lt;T&gt;</code> produces a value without taking
                an input.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>
                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Supplier&lt;Double&gt; randomValue =
    () -&gt; Math.random();

System.out.println(
    randomValue.get()
);</code></pre>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- METHOD REFERENCE -->
        <section class="mb-5">
            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Method References</h3>

            <p>
                Method references provide an even shorter syntax when an
                existing method already matches the required functional
                interface.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>
                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;String&gt; names =
    List.of(
        "Amit",
        "Neha",
        "Rahul"
    );

names.forEach(
    System.out::println
);</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Amit
Neha
Rahul</code></pre>
</div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The expression <code>System.out::println</code> is a method
                reference.
            </p>
        </section>

        <!-- LAMBDA WITH COLLECTION -->
        <section class="mb-5">
            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Lambda with Collections</h3>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>
                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;String&gt; names =
    new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

names.add("Amit");
names.add("Neha");
names.add("Rahul");

names.forEach(
    name -&gt;
        System.out.println(name)
);</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Amit
Neha
Rahul</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>

        <!-- SORTING -->
        <section class="mb-5">
            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Lambda for Sorting</h3>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>
                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;Integer&gt; numbers =
    new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(
        List.of(50, 10, 40, 20)
    );

numbers.sort(
    (a, b) -&gt; Integer.compare(a, b)
);

System.out.println(numbers);</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>[10, 20, 40, 50]</code></pre>
</div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                For simple natural ordering, Java also provides
                <code>Comparator.naturalOrder()</code>.
            </p>
        </section>

        <!-- STREAM -->
        <section class="mb-5">
            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Lambda Expressions with Streams</h3>

            <p>
                Lambda expressions are widely used with the Stream API.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>
                <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;Integer&gt; numbers =
    List.of(
        10, 15, 20, 25, 30
    );

numbers.stream()
    .filter(number -&gt; number &gt; 20)
    .forEach(
        number -&gt;
            System.out.println(number)
    );</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>25
30</code></pre>
</div>
        </section>

        <!-- CAPTURE -->
        <section class="mb-5">
            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Variable Capture</h3>

            <p>
                A lambda can access local variables from its enclosing scope
                when those variables are final or effectively final.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>
                <pre class="mb-0"><code>int multiplier = 10;

Function&lt;Integer, Integer&gt; multiply =
    number -&gt; number * multiplier;

System.out.println(
    multiply.apply(5)
);</code></pre>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>50</code></pre>
</div>

            <div class="alert alert-info mt-3">
                <strong>Important:</strong>
                You cannot later reassign <code>multiplier</code> if it is
                being captured by the lambda.
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- THIS -->
        <section class="mb-5">
            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">this in Lambda Expressions</h3>

            <p>
                A lambda expression does not create a new <code>this</code>
                context. Inside a lambda, <code>this</code> refers to the
                enclosing object.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>
                <pre class="mb-0"><code>class Printer {

    private String prefix = "Message: ";

    void print() {

        Consumer&lt;String&gt; action =
            text -&gt;
                System.out.println(
                    prefix + text
                );

        action.accept("Hello");

    }

}</code></pre>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- LAMBDA VS ANONYMOUS -->
        <section class="mb-5">
            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Lambda vs Anonymous Class
            </h3>

            <div class="code-block">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>
                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Runnable task =
    new Runnable() {

        @@Override
        public void run() {

            System.out.println(
                "Running"
            );

        }
    };</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block mt-3">
                <div class="code-lang-tag">Java</div>
                <pre class="mb-0"><code>Runnable task =
    () -&gt;
        System.out.println(
            "Running"
        );</code></pre>
            </div>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The lambda form is more concise when the target type is a
                functional interface.
            </p>
        </section>

        <!-- WHEN TO USE -->
        <section class="mb-5">
            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">When Should You Use Lambda Expressions?</h3>

            <ul>
                <li class="mb-2">
                    Processing collections.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Filtering and transforming stream data.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Passing small pieces of behavior to APIs.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Implementing functional interfaces.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Creating concise callbacks and event-like operations.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <!-- BEST PRACTICES -->
        <section class="mb-5">
            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Lambda Best Practices</h3>

            <ul>
                <li class="mb-2">
                    Keep lambda expressions short and readable.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Use meaningful parameter names.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Prefer method references when they improve readability.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Avoid deeply nested lambda expressions.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Use a regular method when the lambda becomes too complex.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <!-- INTERVIEW -->
        <section class="mb-5">
            <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">Interview Questions</h3>

            <div class="accordion" id="lambdaQuestions">

                <div class="accordion-item">
                    <h2 class="accordion-header">
                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#lambdaQuestionOne">
                            What is a lambda expression?
                        </button>
                    </h2>

                    <div id="lambdaQuestionOne"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#lambdaQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">
                            A lambda expression is a concise representation of
                            behavior that can be used where a compatible
                            functional interface is expected.
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="accordion-item">
                    <h2 class="accordion-header">
                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#lambdaQuestionTwo">
                            What is a functional interface?
                        </button>
                    </h2>

                    <div id="lambdaQuestionTwo"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#lambdaQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">
                            A functional interface has exactly one abstract
                            method and can be used as the target type of a
                            lambda expression.
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="accordion-item">
                    <h2 class="accordion-header">
                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#lambdaQuestionThree">
                            What is the difference between a lambda and an anonymous class?
                        </button>
                    </h2>

                    <div id="lambdaQuestionThree"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#lambdaQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">
                            A lambda is a concise implementation of a
                            functional interface, while an anonymous class
                            creates an anonymous class instance and can define
                            more structure and behavior.
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="accordion-item">
                    <h2 class="accordion-header">
                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#lambdaQuestionFour">
                            What are Predicate, Function, Consumer and Supplier?
                        </button>
                    </h2>

                    <div id="lambdaQuestionFour"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#lambdaQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">
                            Predicate tests a condition, Function transforms
                            input into output, Consumer accepts input without
                            returning a result, and Supplier produces a value
                            without an input.
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="accordion-item">
                    <h2 class="accordion-header">
                        <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                                type="button"
                                data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                                data-bs-target="#lambdaQuestionFive">
                            What is a method reference?
                        </button>
                    </h2>

                    <div id="lambdaQuestionFive"
                         class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                         data-bs-parent="#lambdaQuestions">

                        <div class="accordion-body">
                            A method reference is a compact syntax such as
                            <code>System.out::println</code> for referring to
                            an existing compatible method.
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- SUMMARY -->
        <section class="mb-4">
            <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">
                <h5 class="fw-bold">Summary</h5>

                <p class="mb-0">
                    Lambda expressions provide concise implementations of
                    functional interfaces. They are heavily used with
                    collections, Comparator, the Stream API, and the
                    java.util.function package. Method references provide
                    another concise way to reuse existing methods.
                </p>
            </div>
        </section>

